To wrap up a C program, you simply return from the main function or call exit(). Here's how it works:  

When your main() function finishes its job—whether by hitting the closing brace or hitting a return statement—you're effectively ending the program. For example:  
```c  
int main() {  
    // Your code here  
    return 0; // This exits cleanly  
}  
```  
Or, if you need to terminate mid-execution (like after an error), use `exit(0)` from `stdlib.h`. But honestly? Most of the time, just letting main() naturally finish is the cleanest way to wrap things up.  

Think of it like blowing the final whistle after the match—no extra fluff, just a smooth exit. You’ve got this! 🏆
